{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012PC1toUbhKLjWZyr8RjtyJ","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (29.5KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M86SER3GGCMYY94N0NS262SC/4b7732c0-b3d0-4977-ba67-4df51312e3d6/tool-results/bu6qcp2x3.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n# ard controller image: the static ard binary plus the cve scanner's tools, so\n# `ard serve` in a container can run the cve role as a host subprocess.\n# Published as ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard.\n#\n# Builds for both platforms on a host without QEMU/binfmt: every RUN is in a\n# stage on $BUILDPLATFORM (Go cross-compiles, per-arch binaries are downloaded\n# with checksums), and the final stage only copies files in.\n#\n# The base is trixie, not bookworm: git 2.39 breaks the committer's fetches (#163).\n#\n# Tool versions and checksums are the cve scanner's\n# (internal/roles/scanners/cve/Dockerfile); keep them in step.\n#\n#   docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 \\\n#     --build-arg VERSION=$(git describe --tags --always) \\\n#     --build-arg REVISION=$(git rev-parse HEAD) .\n\nARG GO_IMAGE=golang:1.27.4-trixie@sha256:f18c3d73a184fb0582828510722e723b63a93f7188d11dd223d9be6f6880e65d\n\nFROM --platform=$BUILDPLATFORM ${GO_IMAGE} AS build\nARG TARGETOS\nARG TARGETARCH\nARG VERSION=dev\nARG GOVULNCHECK_VERSION=v1.10.0\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY go.mod go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY . .\nRUN mkdir -p /out && \\\n    CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=${TARGETOS} GOARCH=${TARGETARCH} \\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Builds the root Dockerfile and checks the image:\n#   - one build for linux/amd64 + linux/arm64 into an OCI layout: the index lists exactly those two platforms\n#   - the native platform loaded into Docker: ard version, the tools at their pinned versions, uid, labels,\n#     and the cve scanner run in the image as a plain subprocess (how `ard serve` runs it in a container)\n# Needs Docker with buildx and network (base image, module proxy, osv.dev). Prints PASS:/FAIL: per case.\n# Exit 0 ok, 1 a case failed, 2 tooling.\nset -uo pipefail\n\nROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")/..\" && pwd)\"\nBUILDER=\"${ARD_IMAGE_TEST_BUILDER:-ard-image-test}\"\ncommand -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo \"ERROR: docker is required\" >&2; exit 2; }\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f055458-edd7-4d37-90f5-92083b2f6320","uuid":"464ac313-fb52-4aed-b7c7-76585591810f","timestamp":"2026-11-24T12:46:00.788Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# ard controller image: the static ard binary plus the cve scanner's tools, so\n# `ard serve` in a container can run the cve role as a host subprocess.\n# Published as ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard.\n#\n# Builds for both platforms on a host without QEMU/binfmt: every RUN is in a\n# stage on $BUILDPLATFORM (Go cross-compiles, per-arch binaries are downloaded\n# with checksums), and the final stage only copies files in.\n#\n# The base is trixie, not bookworm: git 2.39 breaks the committer's fetches (#163).\n#\n# Tool versions and checksums are the cve scanner's\n# (internal/roles/scanners/cve/Dockerfile); keep them in step.\n#\n#   docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 \\\n#     --build-arg VERSION=$(git describe --tags --always) \\\n#     --build-arg REVISION=$(git rev-parse HEAD) .\n\nARG GO_IMAGE=golang:1.27.4-trixie@sha256:f18c3d73a184fb0582828510722e723b63a93f7188d11dd223d9be6f6880e65d\n\nFROM --platform=$BUILDPLATFORM ${GO_IMAGE} AS build\nARG TARGETOS\nARG TARGETARCH\nARG VERSION=dev\nARG GOVULNCHECK_VERSION=v1.10.0\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY go.mod go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY . .\nRUN mkdir -p /out && \\\n    CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=${TARGETOS} GOARCH=${TARGETARCH} \\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Builds the root Dockerfile and checks the image:\n#   - one build for linux/amd64 + linux/arm64 into an OCI layout: the index lists exactly those two platforms\n#   - the native platform loaded into Docker: ard version, the tools at their pinned versions, uid, labels,\n#     and the cve scanner run in the image as a plain subprocess (how `ard serve` runs it in a container)\n# Needs Docker with buildx and network (base image, module proxy, osv.dev). Prints PASS:/FAIL: per case.\n# Exit 0 ok, 1 a case failed, 2 tooling.\nset -uo pipefail\n\nROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")/..\" && pwd)\"\nBUILDER=\"${ARD_IMAGE_TEST_BUILDER:-ard-image-test}\"\ncommand -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo \"ERROR: docker is required\" >&2; exit 2; }\ndocker buildx version >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo \"ERROR: docker buildx is required\" >&2; exit 2; }\ncommand -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo \"ERROR: jq is required\" >&2; exit 2; }\n\nwork=\"$(mktemp -d)\" || { echo \"ERROR: mktemp failed\" >&2; exit 2; }\ntag=\"ard-image-test:$$\"\ntag2=\"ard-image-test:$$-v\"\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$work\"; docker image rm -f \"$tag\" \"$tag2\" >/dev/null 2>&1' EXIT\nfailures=0\npass() { echo \"PASS: $1\"; }\nfail() { echo \"FAIL: $1${2:+ - $2}\" >&2; failures=$((failures + 1)); }\n\ndocker buildx inspect \"$BUILDER\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || docker buildx create --name \"$BUILDER\" --driver docker-container >/dev/null \\\n  || { echo \"ERROR: cannot create buildx builder $BUILDER\" >&2; exit 2; }\n\nversion=\"$(git -C \"$ROOT\" describe --tags --always)\"\nrevision=\"$(git -C \"$ROOT\" rev-parse HEAD)\"\ncase \"$(docker info --format '{{.Architecture}}')\" in\n  aarch64|arm64) native=linux/arm64 ;;\n  x86_64|amd64) native=linux/amd64 ;;\n  *) echo \"ERROR: unknown docker architecture\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\nesac\n\n# ---- both platforms, as ard-build builds them ----------------------------------------------------------------------\nif ! docker buildx build --builder \"$BUILDER\" --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 \\\n     --build-arg VERSION=\"$version\" --build-arg REVISION=\"$revision\" \\\n     --output \"type=oci,dest=$work/index.tar\" \"$ROOT\" >\"$work/multi.log\" 2>&1; then\n  tail -30 \"$work/multi.log\" >&2; echo \"ERROR: multi-platform build failed\" >&2; exit 1\nfi\nmkdir \"$work/oci\" && tar -xf \"$work/index.tar\" -C \"$work/oci\"\nidx=\"$work/oci/blobs/$(jq -r '.manifests[0].digest | sub(\":\"; \"/\")' \"$work/oci/index.json\")\"\nplatforms=\"$(jq -r '[.manifests[] | select(.platform.os != \"unknown\") | .platform.os + \"/\" + .platform.architecture] | sort | join(\",\")' \"$idx\")\"\nif [ \"$platforms\" = \"linux/amd64,linux/arm64\" ]; then pass \"image index has exactly linux/amd64 and linux/arm64\"\nelse fail \"image index has exactly linux/amd64 and linux/arm64\" \"got $platforms\"; fi\n\n# ---- native platform, loaded --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nif ! docker buildx build --builder \"$BUILDER\" --platform \"$native\" --load -t \"$tag\" \\\n     --build-arg VERSION=\"$version\" --build-arg REVISION=\"$revision\" \"$ROOT\" >\"$work/native.log\" 2>&1; then\n  tail -30 \"$work/native.log\" >&2; echo \"ERROR: native build failed\" >&2; exit 1\nfi\nrun() { docker run --rm \"$@\"; }\n\nout=\"$(run \"$tag\" version 2>&1)\"\nif [ \"$out\" = \"ard $version\" ]; then pass \"ard version prints the stamped version\"\nelse fail \"ard version prints the stamped version\" \"got '$out', want 'ard $version'\"; fi\n\ntools_ok() {\n  run --entrypoint go \"$tag\" version | grep -q '^go version go1\\.27\\.4 ' || { echo \"go: $(run --entrypoint go \"$tag\" version)\" >&2; return 1; }\n  run --entrypoint govulncheck \"$tag\" -version 2>&1 | grep -q 'govulncheck@v1\\.10\\.0' || { echo \"govulncheck: $(run --entrypoint govulncheck \"$tag\" -version 2>&1 | head -3)\" >&2; return 1; }\nVERSION ?= $(shell git describe --tags --always --dirty 2>/dev/null || echo dev)\nLDFLAGS := -s -w -X main.version=$(VERSION)\n\n.PHONY: build test lint fmt clean cve-fixtures lint-scanner-fixtures chart-test image-test\n\nbuild:\n\tCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags \"$(LDFLAGS)\" -o bin/ard ./cmd/ard\n\ntest:\n\tgo test ./...\n\nlint:\n\tgo vet ./...\n\t@out=\"$$(gofmt -l .)\"; if [ -n \"$$out\" ]; then echo \"gofmt needed:\"; echo \"$$out\"; exit 1; fi\n\nfmt:\n\tgofmt -w .\n\n# Renders the two Argo CD charts under deploy/ and checks them (needs helm and yq;\n# run by the ard-ci pipeline, not part of `make test`).\nchart-test:\n\tdeploy/test-charts.sh\n\n# Builds the ard image for both platforms and checks it (needs Docker buildx and\n# network; not part of `make test`).\nimage-test:\n\tdeploy/test-image.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real cve scanner fixtures inside the pinned tool image.\n# Needs Docker and network; not part of `make test`.\ncve-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-cve-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/cve\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-cve-fixtures sh testdata/cve/regen.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real lint scanner fixtures in the pinned image (see\n# internal/roles/testdata/lint/regen.sh). Needs Docker; not part of `make test`.\nlint-scanner-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-lint-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/lint\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-lint-fixtures sh testdata/lint/regen.sh\n\nclean:\n\trm -rf bin\ninternal/agent/claude/testdata/CAPTURE.md:4:CLI (`TestedVersion`). The `synthetic_*` files stand in until a human commits\ninternal/agent/claude/testdata/CAPTURE.md:17:claude --version                          # must equal TestedVersion; otherwise stop\ninternal/agent/claude/real_test.go:118:\t\t\t\tif !r.OK || r.Subtype != \"success\" || r.CostUSD == nil || r.LimitHit != nil || r.CLIVersion != TestedVersion || r.SessionID != \"session-1\" {\ninternal/agent/claude/cli_integration_test.go:5:// them on every TestedVersion bump (including the OAuth token and API key\ninternal/agent/claude/cli_integration_test.go:461:// docs/proposal.md, #125), but re-check it on a TestedVersion bump when the\n211:| lts | endoflife.date + manifests: go.mod, package.json, Dockerfile `FROM`, Helm charts | 7 d | 1 per component | yes |\n224:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are always forced after any config, because findings that vanish are autoclosed: `--max-issues-per-linter=0 --max-same-issues=0` (no truncation), `--new=false --new-from-rev= --new-from-merge-base= --new-from-patch=` (a repo config asking for \"new issues only\" would report nothing on a clean checkout), `--issues-exit-code=0 --fix=false --path-mode=abs`, plus JSON output to a temp file. It fails closed: a golangci-lint exit other than 0, a `typecheck` issue (a compile error), a module needing a newer Go than the image's (`GOTOOLCHAIN=local`; toolchain switching is a follow-up), a path outside the repository or with a control character, a malformed baseline, and a missing `baseline` file all exit non-zero with no output. Because the image builds golangci-lint with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, a repo config's `goplugin` custom linter cannot load and fails the run. The fingerprint is `lint:<rule>:<path>:<msg>`. `<rule>` is the linter plus `/<code>` for a check code (`staticcheck/SA4000`, `gosec/G104`), or for `govet` and `revive` an analyzer name (`govet/printf`). `<msg>` drops the code, control characters and repeated whitespace, and replaces every standalone number with `N`, so line numbers and \"complexity 31\" versus \"32\" never move a fingerprint; a fingerprint over 512 bytes has its message cut by runes. Identical fingerprints in one file collapse into one finding listing every line; the known gap is that a new occurrence of an identical message in the same file is hidden while the old one is open or baselined (a count-aware baseline is a follow-up). The group is `<rule>:<directory>`, so with `group_size: 20` issues are 20 per rule and directory. Severity has two levels: `medium` for `govet`, `staticcheck/SA*`, `gosec*`, `bodyclose`, `nilerr`, `nilnesserr`, `rowserrcheck`, `sqlclosecheck` and `durationcheck`, `low` for everything else; golangci-lint's own severity is ignored. Messages, source lines and paths are untrusted: they are rendered only inside `text` fences with ``` neutralised, and inline code spans have backticks replaced. Every `//nolint` directive in a `.go` file is itself a finding (rule `nolint`, group `nolint:<directory>`, `low`), so a worker cannot close a lint issue by suppressing it; for the same reason the starter protects `**/.golangci.{yml,yaml,toml,json}`. The baseline is the human-owned file `baseline` in the role directory (a protected path, so no agent can baseline a finding): `#` comments and blank lines are ignored, every other line is one fingerprint, and a finding whose fingerprint is listed is dropped. `ard init` does not ship one, so the scanner records a failure every 24 h until a human commits it; an empty file means no baseline. `scan --baseline` prints a baseline of all current findings (ignoring any existing one), and the exact Docker command is in the Dockerfile's header; the run logs how many baseline entries are unused. Baselining a finding that is open closes its issue as \"No longer detected\" (completed); to suppress it instead, close the issue as not planned. A golangci-lint version bump can change message text and so every fingerprint: bump the version and regenerate every repo's `baseline` in the same human PR.\n230:**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; history is scanned by `scanners/trufflehog-history`. The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\n283:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n292:| Runtime | the role's `Dockerfile` image under Docker if it has one, else a host subprocess; hard timeout | a pod from the role's `Dockerfile` image, or the default `ard-agent` image; hard timeout |\n333:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (2,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 1,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages and uses no other network, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make test`, and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 1000`, `diff_max_lines: 2000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base.\n351:**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue, comments or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored and mentioned in the body. It runs the repository's own linter and the changed packages' tests with coverage through Bash, writing scratch files outside the worktree, reports only problems on added lines and skips a check it cannot run, saying so; the package manager may fetch declared dependencies and nothing else is downloaded. Critical and high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\n353:**The starter security reviewer (`reviewers/security-review`, #43).** `.ard/reviewers/security-review/prompt.txt` looks for injection, authz gaps, secrets and unsafe dependencies the change introduces or makes reachable, plus other weaknesses. It has the same untrusted inputs, Trust rules (steering text in the change is a blocking `high` finding), write rules and role-file format as eng-review, with `role: security-review` and a `## Checks` body. It never builds, tests or runs the repository's code. It runs, when they are installed: gitleaks over the changed files with `--redact`; gosec on changed Go packages with `-nosec`; and semgrep with `--metrics=off` and `--disable-nosem`, using only a rule file already in the repository that the change does not touch, or the sidecar's. Suppressions the change adds are reviewed as part of the change. It skips a missing tool and says so, never downloads rules or vulnerability data, and never copies a secret it finds into its report (file, line and kind only). Each finding's first line starts with `[injection]`, `[authz]`, `[secret]`, `[dependency]` or `[other]`. Critical and high block, medium only when it is one of the four kinds with a nameable exploit, low and info never; `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review. The starter override is Opus 5.5 (#8) with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`. No Dockerfile: host mode with the #84 warning until #55. Running pinned tools in controller code is #205.\n355:**The starter license reviewer (`reviewers/license-review`, #44).** `.ard/reviewers/license-review/prompt.txt` checks that every dependency a PR adds or upgrades has a license on the owners' allowlist. The allowlist is one SPDX id per line (`#` comments; `A OR B` passes when any operand is listed, `A AND B` only when all are, `A WITH E` only when listed verbatim; no per-package exceptions). It is read from the sidecar's `licenses/allowlist.txt` when the role has a sidecar and that file exists, else from `allowlist.txt` in the role directory (`$ARD_ROLE_DIR`, read from the config snapshot on `main`, under a protected path). An allowlist found in the PR, the diff, the issue or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored, and an unreadable or empty one ends `needs-human`. For Go it runs `go-licenses check` and `go-licenses report` when the tool is on the host `PATH` (optional; it never installs tools), else falls back to `go mod download -json` and the module's LICENSE files; for npm it reads the lockfile `license` field. A license outside the allowlist, or one that cannot be determined, is a blocking `high` finding on the manifest line that adds it (verdict `changes`, so the merger never merges while the label stands); a license that changed but is still allowed is `info`; other ecosystems are `info` and unchecked; no dependency change approves with \"No dependency changes to review\". The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with `when.paths` listing the manifests and lockfiles above. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\n404:├── scanners/<name>/     # scan (executable) or prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n405:├── triage/<name>/       # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n406:├── workers/<name>/      # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n407:├── reviewers/<name>/    # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n412:- A `Dockerfile` builds the role's image; without one the local executor runs a host subprocess and the Kubernetes executor uses the default `ard-agent` image (agent CLIs, git, common linters). A role that reads untrusted input without one triggers the host-mode warning (see Executors and the Reaper).\n456:**Where `ard serve` reads config (decided, #87).** After each fetch into its own clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`, see Local-executor details) `ard serve` resolves `origin/main` to a SHA and reads `config.yaml` and the role directories from that commit, not from the working tree. It exports them into a snapshot at `~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/<sha>/`, kept 24 hours after a newer SHA replaces it, because roles run from a host path: a role directory, its `Dockerfile` and the scan worktree must all describe the one commit the tick's jobs use. Editing the host's working tree therefore changes nothing on a running controller, with one exception: a local `paused: true` is honoured (see the kill switch), and only that key is read locally. A merged change applies on the next tick without a pull. Symlinks and submodules under the role directories are rejected, and a config that cannot be fetched or does not validate counts as paused. If `origin/main` cannot be loaded at startup, `ard serve` starts paused instead of exiting, posts the load error to the status issue once per distinct error, and retries every tick; no loop runs until a config has loaded. `ard config check` validates the working tree, for authoring; `ard config check --ref <ref>` validates a commit of the local checkout (for example `--ref origin/main` after `git fetch`) without fetching.\n467:- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders and fixed CLI prompt text are back; isolation of settings `env` is not pinned by a test, since no Bash call is scripted; Bash can still read anything the process can (host mode, #84); and an agent can rewrite CLAUDE.md or `.claude/` in its worktree, which later runs ignore but a worker PR could carry into the target repo (the `ard init` protected-path defaults cover `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, so such a PR needs a human).\n499:## Delivery\n501:How ard itself is built, published and deployed. Jenkins and Argo CD do the work; the cluster-specific wiring (the Jenkins job definitions and their publishing Jenkinsfiles, credentials, the Argo CD projects and the image pin) lives in the deployment repository, not here. This repository holds only what is ard's: the `Makefile` targets CI runs, `Dockerfile`, `deploy/` and `deploy/cosign.pub`, the key ard images are signed with. `Dockerfile`, `.dockerignore`, `deploy/` and any `Jenkinsfile*` are in ard's `merge.protected_paths`, so the merger never lands a change to them without a human approval.\n504:- **Publish.** A green `main` build starts `ard-build`, whose Jenkinsfile is not in this repository, so nothing merged here can reach its credentials. It builds the `Dockerfile` for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64` (no emulation: every `RUN` cross-compiles or downloads on the build platform), sign","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M86SER3GGCMYY94N0NS262SC/4b7732c0-b3d0-4977-ba67-4df51312e3d6/tool-results/bu6qcp2x3.txt","persistedOutputSize":30179},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012PC1toUbhKLjWZyr8RjtyJ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
